Fixing your production failure — inspecting the incident and running the repro.
I've confirmed the crash — now tracing how nested prefixes are joined.
I found the likely cause in the prefix joining logic — applying a targeted fix.
The initial fix resolves the reported case — now verifying no regressions.
Root cause: `buildRoutePrefix` in `lib/plugin-override.js` unconditionally prepended `/` to a child prefix lacking a leading slash, even when the parent prefix already ended with `/`. `prefix "/api/"` + `prefix "v1"` produced `/api//v1`, so the route was registered as `/api//v1/orders` and `GET /api/v1/orders` fell through to the 404 handler (surfaced as 500 `E_PREFIX_JOIN` by the incident app).

Files changed:
- `lib/plugin-override.js:85` — changed `else if (pluginPrefix[0] !== '/')` to `else if (!instancePrefix.endsWith('/') && pluginPrefix[0] !== '/')`, so a separator `/` is only added when neither side supplies one. The double-slash strip case is unchanged.

Validation performed:
- Manual inject repro: before fix `printRoutes` showed `/api//v1/orders` and `GET /api/v1/orders` → 404; after fix shows `/api/v1/orders` → 200.
- Executable incident `/work/repro`: before `ORIGINAL_FAILURE_REPRODUCED (500)`, after `ORIGINAL_FAILURE_ABSENT (200)`.
- Matrix of 8 outer/inner slash combinations (with/without leading/trailing `/`, root `/`, empty) all resolve to single-slash path → 200.
- Existing tests green: `route-prefix.test.js` (26 pass), `register/plugin.1-4` (51 pass), `404s/route.2/route.6` (93 pass).

Remaining uncertainty: none for this incident; prefix params/trailing-slash semantics (`/api/v1/` vs `/api/v1`) still follow existing `route.js`/`prefixTrailingSlash` handling and were not exhaustively re-verified beyond the matrix above.

